  {
   "type": "faq",
   "h": "Questions validation and engineering teams ask",
   "items": [
    {
     "q": "You are a new supplier. How do we check still fabrication quality before we order?",
     "a": "Ask for the sample turnover package. It shows the fabrication dossier format: weld map, weld log, borescope records, material certificates by heat number and the pressure vessel documents. Your QA team can audit [the shop that builds the still] and witness its hold points, and your quality unit approves the FAT protocol before we run it."
    },
    {
     "q": "Which FAT and turnover documents keep our validation team on schedule?",
     "a": "The documentation list on this page is what ships with the still. The FAT protocol reaches you [protocol lead time, weeks] before the test for review, the executed report and deviation log before the still ships, and the IQ/OQ protocols with the still, so your team is not writing them during start-up."
    },
    {
     "q": "Why distil when Ph. Eur. now allows membrane WFI?",
     "a": "Since April 2017, Ph. Eur. 0169 accepts reverse osmosis coupled with other suitable techniques, and USP has long accepted any process equivalent or superior to distillation. Distillation still makes sense when you want hot WFI for a hot loop, when your contamination control strategy relies on the longest inspection record, or when steam or cheap power is on hand. Membrane WFI saves energy where ambient storage suits your process. We model both on your data when the choice is close."
    },
    {
     "q": "How much endotoxin reduction can we count on?",
     "a": "Only what the still manufacturer guarantees, which FDA's inspection guide puts at 2.5 to 3 log for many stills. A 3 log still fed at 250 EU/mL would deliver WFI at 0.25 EU/mL, which already fails the < 0.25 EU/mL limit. So we set an endotoxin target for the PW feed well below that, and design the PW system and its sanitization to hold it."
    },
    {
     "q": "What feed water does the still need?",
     "a": "PW for a multi-effect still, plus the manufacturer's limits on silica and hardness, which scale hot surfaces, and on chloride, which attacks stainless steel at still temperatures. Vapour compression stills often accept softened or RO feed. We write the feed limits into the functional specification and size the PW skid to meet them."
    },
    {
     "q": "Does an imported still need a Canadian registration number?",
     "a": "Yes. Pressure vessels and fittings installed in Canada need a CRN under CSA B51 from the province of installation, and a still designed for another market may need design changes before it is accepted. We include registration in the project schedule from the start. [Confirm Aquemi's CRN process and the provinces covered.]"
    },
    {
     "q": "What happens at start-up and shutdown?",
     "a": "Every start-up diverts distillate to drain until conductivity and temperature reach setpoint. The condenser and distillate line drain on shutdown. FDA's inspection guide describes a still whose condenser held about 50 litres of WFI for up to several days between runs, a source of endotoxin; we design so that cannot happen."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can the still feed a hot loop directly?",
     "a": "Yes. Distillate leaves hot and fills a tank and loop circulating above 70 °C, the example Annex 1 paragraph 6.10 gives. Users who need cooler WFI draw it through point-of-use coolers. Storage and distribution covers the loop design."
    },
    {
     "q": "Do hot WFI systems rouge, and who deals with it?",
     "a": "Hot WFI and pure steam systems develop rouge, an iron oxide film on stainless steel, over time. We record the surface condition at handover and include inspection in the service plan, with derouging and repassivation when trends call for it."
    },
    {
     "q": "Who builds the still?",
